"""Carefully composed concurrency mechanisms for Unit 4.3."""
from atlas_figures import shell
LESSONS=[{"title":"Shared value or explicit message","text":"Two threads can read the same in-process object, so access needs coordination when either writes. Two processes can exchange an explicit message instead; message passing avoids shared application-memory writes but introduces delivery and ordering questions."},{"title":"How one increment gets lost","text":"Both threads observe 7 before either writes. Each calculates 8, and both store 8. The result is 8 rather than 9. The race occurs because reading, adding, and writing are not one atomic operation."},{"title":"Which coordination tool solves which problem?","text":"A mutex gives one owner at a time. A semaphore tracks a finite number of permits. An atomic operation makes a supported individual update indivisible. A condition variable allows a thread to sleep until a predicate may be true, and must be checked again after waking."},{"title":"A deadlock has a circular wait","text":"Thread A owns lock X and waits for Y. Thread B owns Y and waits for X. Neither can advance, because neither releases its first lock. Acquiring locks in a consistent global order can prevent this particular cycle."}]
